{"id": 8717036, "name": "Jones v. Massey", "name_abbreviation": "Jones v. Massey", "decision_date": "1881-07-21", "docket_number": "No. 1063", "first_page": "615", "last_page": "616", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "15 S.C. 615"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [], "analysis": {"cardinality": 100, "char_count": 883, "ocr_confidence": 0.518, "sha256": "fc505a12c0d0fff73946654f2be42bb09a48bdf6fdfd97f8e1bf232ed4248a9b", "simhash": "1:43016906ca914b89", "word_count": 153}, "last_updated": "2023-07-14T20:32:19.690436+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["Jones v. Massey."], "opinions": [{"text": "After the decision of this court, reported in 14 8. O. 292, Judge Mackey passed an order restraining the sheriff of Lancaster county from putting James B,. Massey into possession of the lands which he had elected to take under his mother\u2019s will. From that order this appeal was taken. Held, that Judge Mackey had misconstrued the opinion of this court, iii regarding the possession of this land as part of the statu quo intended to be preserved; that Massey\u2019s right to the land was not questioned, nor was that portion of the Circuit decree which ordered the possession to be restored to him interfered with; that Massey\u2019s right to the possession of this land has been fully adjudicated, and he should no longer be deprived of its enjoyment. OPINION by\nJuly 21st, 1881.\nErnest Moore, for appellant. R. E. Allison, contra.\nSimpson, C. J.,", "type": "majority", "author": "Simpson, C. J.,"}], "attorneys": ["Ernest Moore, for appellant. R. E. Allison, contra."], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "No. 1063.\nJones v. Massey.\nApril Term, 1881."}, "file_name": "0615-02", "first_page_order": 625, "last_page_order": 626}